  "behavior": [
    "CellFilterCell<T> owns the same private CellRecord<T> used as the real MapNode key, with a BaseAllocator handle outside native record fields. Its target chain is the existing CellObjectNode<T> graph, never a detached list or metadata mirror.",
    "Actual CellMap insertion calls shared CellRecord::move_construct on the native temporary lookup cell. Actual map hasher/equality call shared CellRecord hash_code/is_equal. Point construction uses the same CellIndex conversion implementation as public point operations.",
    "Index-copy construction owns independent coordinates. Move construction empties the source index/head and transfers the actual target nodes without extra Clone or Drop. Inline indices copy into destination storage; heap pointers transfer.",
    "Explicit move_from_discard preserves native LocalArray move-assignment storage policy, including heap-to-heap pointer exchange leaving the source empty with the old destination index allocation. It transfers the source head and skips Target destruction for overwritten destination objects.",
    "Explicit move_self_discard leaves index length/storage untouched and clears the head without Target destruction, matching native Cell self assignment rather than the LocalArray self-assignment no-op alone.",
    "Discard deliberately skips user Drop and can leak target-owned resources. Rust walks discarded links only to release typed allocation leases. Incremental arena block bytes remain retained; the measured i32 Object-first node consumes16 bytes and a before/node/after raw allocation probe advances24 bytes on both native and Rust.",
    "Ordinary standalone destruction and the actual map owner share CellRecord::drop_objects: newest-first Target destruction, original head retained across callbacks, then head clearing and lease release. Tests verify target copy/drop order and existing map callback-phase tests continue passing.",
    "Cell equality and CellHasher compare/hash only native coordinate bytes, ignoring object bookkeeping and payload equality traits. Index lengths participate in equality; rebased anisotropic size arrays construct the measured same index.",
    "Explicit moves between standalone adapters adopt the source creating allocator for transferred nodes; the emptied source retains its own handle. Allocator leases are Rust ownership metadata outside repr(C) CellRecord/Object node fields."
  ],
